==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|En-au-take a powder.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|take<,,took,taken> a powder}}

# {{lb|en|idiomatic|US|colloquial}} To leave without saying goodbye; leave quietly, [[run away]]; [[scram]]; [[depart]] without [[take one's leave|taking leave]] or [[notify]]ing anyone, often with a [[connotation]] of [[avoid]]ing something [[unpleasant]] or [[shirk]]ing [[responsibility]].
#: {{synonyms|en|take a walkout powder}}
#* '''1933''', Raymond Chandler, ''Blackmailers Don't Shoot'', ''Collected Stories'', Everyman's Library (2002), p. 20:
#*: Macdonald spoke slowly, bitterly. "The kidnapping is one too many for me, Costello. I don't want any part of it. I'm '''takin' a powder''' from this toy mob. I took a chance that bright boy might side me."
#* {{RQ:Mezzrow Blues|page=66|passage=First Mrs Hitchcock packed up and '''took a powder''', and there was hell to pay.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1971|author=Louis-Ferdinand D. Celine|title=Death on the Installment Plan|page=446|passage=Our idea was that once the storm had subsided we'd '''take a powder''' one night with our dough. . . We'd take our stuff and give ourselves a change of air. . . move to a different neighborhood.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1979|author=Dan McCall|title=Beecher: A Novel|page=162|passage="Mr. Tilton said you told him you would '''take a powder'''." "'''Take a powder'''?" said Henry. "I once heard a man from Nevada tell me he would '''take a powder''', meaning he was leaving town."}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2000|author=Barbara Weltman|title=The Complete Idiot's Guide to Starting a Home-Based Business|page=271|passage=But when you suffer losses, Uncle Sam may '''take a powder'''.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2004|author=Robert Hough|author2=Hogie Wyckoff|title=The Final Confession of Mabel Stark|page=418
|passage=Go on, now. Scram. '''Take a powder'''. And don't come back till people on the street start wishing you a good afternoon.}}

